antibiotics we use them frequently to  treat bacterial infections and they are  very good at their job however concerns  have been rising about the creation of  superbugs which are fatal bacteria that  are resistant to our arsenal of  antibiotics  what is antibiotic resistance and how do  bacteria become resistant to the  antibiotics we choose to kill them in  this video we will be focusing on a  strain of antibiotics known as amino  glycosides which work by inhibiting the  function of a bacterial cells ribosomes  ribosomes are organelles which make  proteins they work by mashing sections  of mRNA containing the cell's genetic  code with matching sections of tRNA  which carry amino acids creating a  protein the ribosome itself is made up  of RNA and proteins the 30s ribosomal  subunit is a section of our RNA which  ensures that only the strongest codon  anticodon pairs can bond antibiotics  bind to and alter the xxx structure  allowing for weaker and correct parents  to be formed the wrong protein is formed  and the bacterial cell is either stopped  from growing or killed some bacteria  however have developed enzymes known as  methyl transferases which add methyl  groups to specific bases in the our RNA  the addition of a methyl group makes the  pocket where the antibiotic normally  binds to small which allows ribosomal  function to continue as normal  3d models are methylated our RNA can be  found on the PDB website here we see a  section of our RNA where a methyl group  has been added to a blondie here we see  similar structure added to an adenine  other methods of antibiotic resistance  exist as well in one method bacterial  enzymes add chains and molecules to  antibiotics which one activates them  another method bacteria develops pumps  which expel into biotics from within the  cell and yet another method bacterial  cells develop less permeable cell  membranes which stops antibiotics from  entering antibiotic overuse is the  biggest cause of antibiotic resistance  killing weaker bacterial strains allows  mutated strains to spread an antibiotic  resistance epidemic is in our future of  antibiotics keeping rendered ineffective  however it's not too late according to  the World Health Organization you can  help slow antibiotic resistance by only  using antibiotics when prescribed never  sharing antibiotics and regularly  washing your hands to prevent infection  if you want to learn more about  antibiotics and antibiotic resistance  feel free to visit the PDB website  thanks for watching
 

